## Bug: `focus()` doesn't scroll horizontally for wide-glyph (double-width) lines, leaving the cursor off-screen

### Summary

`Editor::focus()`'s horizontal-scroll decision compares the cursor's raw **character-count** column against the viewport's **terminal-cell** width. For lines made of double-width glyphs (CJK, emoji, etc.), the character count under-estimates the actual display width, so `focus()` can conclude no scroll is needed while the cursor is actually off-screen. `get_visible_cursor()` computes the correct grapheme-width-based visual column and (correctly) reports the cursor as not visible in this case — the two functions disagree because only one of them accounts for wide glyphs.

### Repro (0.0.6, `default-features = false, features = ["crossterm"]`, and confirmed identical on current `master`)

```rust
use ratatui::layout::Rect;
use ratatui_code_editor::editor::Editor;

fn area() -> Rect { Rect::new(0, 0, 40, 10) }

#[test]
fn wide_glyph_cursor_should_stay_visible() {
    let line: String = std::iter::repeat('界').take(20).collect();
    let mut ed = Editor::new("text", &line, vec![]).unwrap();
    ed.set_cursor(line.chars().count()); // cursor at the end
    ed.focus(&area());

    assert_eq!(ed.get_offset_x(), 0); // never scrolled
    assert!(ed.get_visible_cursor(&area()).is_none()); // cursor is off-screen
}
```

`area` is 40 cells wide; the line number gutter takes ~7, leaving ~33 visible columns. The cursor's character column is 20 (`< 33`), so `focus()`'s check `col >= self.offset_x + visible_width` never trips and `offset_x` stays `0`. But the line's actual display width is 40 cells (each `界` is 2 cells wide), so the true cursor position is well past the visible area. `get_visible_cursor()` (which *does* sum grapheme widths correctly) reports `None`.

Plain-ASCII lines and combining-mark lines don't trigger this: for those, character count is always `>=` the true visual width per character, so the same char-based comparison in `focus()` happens to over-scroll rather than under-scroll, and the cursor stays visible by luck rather than by correct accounting.

### Suggested fix direction

In `Editor::focus()`'s `offset_x` adjustment (`src/editor.rs`), compute the cursor's visual column the same way `get_visible_cursor()` already does — summing `grapheme_width` over the line's graphemes up to the cursor — instead of using the raw character index (`col = self.cursor - self.code.line_to_char(line)`). The scroll-target math (`col.saturating_sub(step_size)` etc.) would then need to work in the same visual-column space, and `offset_x` would need to be resolved back to a character index consistently with how `get_visible_cursor()` already interprets it (as a character index that gets grapheme-summed) — i.e. finding the character offset whose visual column is closest to the desired scroll target, rather than assigning a visual-column value directly into a field that's read elsewhere as a character count.

### Context

Found while evaluating `ratatui-code-editor` as an embedded editor foundation for a terminal game (`slaptijack/human-exception`). Not a blocker for adoption there — recorded as a documented, accepted limitation for now (source content and plain-ASCII/combining-mark editing are unaffected; only cursor-visibility-follows-viewport for long wide-glyph lines is affected) — but worth fixing since it silently loses the cursor rather than failing loudly.
